Suttanipāta I–III: poetry, conduct, and renunciation (@buddhist-snp-first-three: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @buddhism, #source @buddhist-source-suttanipata, #philosophy @buddhist-snp-octads-and-beyond, #buddhism @buddhist-snp-octads-and-beyond) { r_5PVDPVSXQ3WNF32YA1FKQZ4B3G
**Read:** the introduction and chapters I–III of the discourse text in [The Suttanipāta](../sources/Suttanipata.lingua#r_2PQ69G8GRX5WRVMFS1VYZKXQMS): the Serpent, Minor, and Great chapters. Use the guide to the suttas and consult commentary where a passage is difficult.

Poetry works through images, contrasts, compression, and voice as well as explicit argument. Explain how a verse presents conduct, renunciation, or release before paraphrasing it as a doctrine. The vocabulary learned from the Nikāyas helps formulate questions; it should not prevent a passage from challenging your first interpretation.

**Compare:** take an image of attachment or letting go and examine its surrounding verses. Give two plausible paraphrases, identify what each explains, and describe what remains uncertain. Separate the verse, its traditional commentary, and Bodhi’s explanation.

**Check:** explain why understanding a poem requires more than supplying a dictionary meaning for each word. Carry that discipline into the Octads and the final questions.

**Continue:** [Suttanipāta IV–V: views and the questions beyond](SNP-Octads-and-beyond.lingua#r_3N0CA3FMXHET5AA7YMF998RRCW).

**Return:** [Buddhism: from the path to sustained inquiry](../Buddhism.lingua#r_7P61Z6HHZNZZ8ND3DHXSFSNRRE).
